      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The UN and similar international bodies often struggle to prioritize specific national interests due to the concept of state sovereignty, which legally prioritizes a nation's right to self-determination above global intervention. This structure frequently leads to decision-making deadlocks in the Security Council, where powerful member states use veto power to shield their own political and economic interests. Furthermore, the inherent consensus-driven nature of global governance often results in politically agreeable, yet ineffectual, \"best-of-all-worlds\" resolutions that benefit no one decisively.",
